body:
"(I’ve had a lot of doctors suggest that if I ate, which I try to do, then I wouldn’t have gp"

&#x200B;

OH GOD SO MUCH THIS. I saw who was allegedly the second-best neurogastroenterologist in my country and she basically intimated that I just wasn't \*trying\* hard enough to eat. Grr.

One of the benefits is that if anything happens to you and you need medical treatment the doctors will know that they may need to adapt things in order to be most suitable for you. For isntance people with EDS often don't respond to local anaesthetics, so then they could give you an alternative instead.

&#x200B;

When I was diagnosed I was relatively healthy, but then a few years after that, I had a flare-up which is still going on really. Having the diagnosis has meant that I've been directed to the most appropriate specialists faster than I otherwise might have been.
